{
  "$comment": [
    "The 25 consumer smart-home devices this PoC screens.",
    "",
    "This is our own list (the exercise permits it) rather than one of the three",
    "supplied sheets. Rationale is in README.md.",
    "",
    "Field notes:",
    "  legal_name         Primary name passed to /v1/resolution.",
    "  resolution_aliases Fallback names, tried in order when legal_name resolves",
    "                     poorly. This exists because Ring does not resolve under",
    "                     'Ring LLC' at all -- every candidate is a piston-ring",
    "                     manufacturer. It resolves under its pre-Amazon legal name,",
    "                     'Bot Home Automation, Inc.'. Assume this is common, not rare.",
    "  hq_address         Publicly known headquarters, used as a resolution hint.",
    "                     Street-level where confidently known, city-level otherwise.",
    "                     Sayari's own docs note that without an address or identifier",
    "                     every match is scored 'weak', so this materially improves",
    "                     resolution quality.",
    "  parent             Corporate parent where the brand is not its own top company.",
    "                     Recorded as context, never asserted as a finding -- ownership",
    "                     findings must come from the graph, not from this file.",
    "  reject_patterns    Case-insensitive substrings that disqualify a candidate even when the brand token matches. Derived from candidates actually returned by the live API, not hypothetical. 'Ring' needs the longest list because RING is a common noun in industrial company names and token matching cannot save us there.",
    "  anchor_entity_ids  Hand-verified entity ids, always included in the family. These pin IDENTITY, not data: every run re-fetches their risk, ownership and shipments live, and resolution still runs alongside and can add entities. The anchor exists because resolution is query-sensitive -- adding an address hint changed EZVIZ's result set and dropped the registry node carrying the Hikvision/CETC chain -- so a refresh can gain findings but cannot silently lose a node."
  ],
